WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.700
Film Courage: I’d love to hear your story
in terms of was there a certain film that

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.900
made you want to be a writer, your choice
in a college major?

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:12.049
Erik Bork, Screenwriter: When I was in high
school the film THE WORLD ACCORDING TO GARP

00:00:12.049 --> 00:00:13.730
had a big impact on me.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:20.070
It was about a writer whose mother was a nonfiction
writer and my mother was also kind of an aspiring

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:24.010
nonfiction writer (very different from that
character).

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:29.759
But still that was the first movie that I
was like there’s something that I related

00:00:29.759 --> 00:00:33.160
to in that movie that was different from any
other movie I’d ever seen.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:38.469
It made me think about ‘Somebody wrote that,’
which was a phrase the Writer’s Guild used

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:41.820
in an ad campaign once to get respect for
writers ‘Somebody wrote that.’

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:46.360
They had all these lines of dialogue on Billboards
‘Don’t forget, a writer wrote that.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.070
An actor didn’t just come up with that.’

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:51.840
But anyway THE WORLD ACCORDING TO GARP had
an impact and I think when I went to college

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:58.120
I knew I wanted to pursue something creative
that involved writing whether it was fiction

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:05.159
or screenwriting or maybe even music and so
after a year or two I settle in on becoming

00:01:05.159 --> 00:01:10.920
a film major and maybe trying to become a
writer/director because a lot of my favorite

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:17.409
movies at that time were writer/director movies
like Woody Allen movies of that era like late

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:20.770
70’s or 80’s.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:28.880
So I pursued being a film major at my undergraduate
in Ohio which is my home state and got a four-year

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:35.220
degree, BFA in motion picture production which
guarantees you a job in the industry immediately

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.230
when you show up with that.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:42.760
I just decided I was going to movie to Los
Angeles and become an assistant because I

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.360
kind of knew you know that whole starting
in the William Morris mailroom kind of thing

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:51.159
that I knew that was the thing you could do
where people had moved out to LA from Dayton,

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:55.649
Ohio where I was from before me who’d gone
to film school before me who had these kind

00:01:55.649 --> 00:01:59.049
of jobs working as assistants at the studios
and stuff.

00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:00.810
So I decided that is going to be my path.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:04.890
I’m going to get a day job will be one of
those kind of jobs where I’ll be like a

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:11.860
secretary and I’ll just be writing on the
side and so that’s what I pursued and was

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:17.500
lucky enough to get into the Fox Studios kind
of in-house temp pool where somebody in the

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.440
Human Resources department had all these temps
that every day they would assign to these

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.320
different jobs throughout the studio where
they needed somebody to fill in for a day

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.910
or a week or a month or whatever because some
assistant was on maternity leave or was sick

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:36.400
or had just left and they hadn’t replaced
them yet so I got my taste of all these different

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.190
jobs through 20th Century Fox and the fox
network.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:44.690
You know everything from Legal Department
to accounting and finance like very corporate

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:49.080
departments but also some of the eventually
the more creative side where you are working

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:54.170
on a TV show for a production company which
is of course what you really want if you’re

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.780
an aspiring writer.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.460
And so I did that for a number of years and
eventually got assigned to Tom Hanks his production

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:06.940
company just as a temp which turned into a
full-time assistant job working there where

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:11.700
it was basically him and his main assistant
who went with him on location and had been

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.420
with him for years and me.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.920
I was like the guy in the office answering
the phone and opening the mail and he was

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.820
off shooting FORREST GUMP for like five months
at one point on the East Coast and I was the

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:26.500
only one there in the office kind of holding
down the fort so to speak at his little production

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.410
company that was based at fox at that time.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:36.400
So while I was doing that I was writing scripts
on the side and I had worked as a writer’s

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.470
assistant on the show PICKET FENCES right
before I worked for Tom Hanks and got to know

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:44.370
other people at my level, other assistants
which is a really helpful thing to do.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:47.870
You know when they say you have to move to
Los Angeles to be a screenwriter you don’t

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.730
have to but it helps.

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:53.540
And one of the main reasons it can help is
if you’re the kind of person that gets a

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:58.290
job in the business like that where you are
around people doing what you want to be doing

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.751
and you meet people at your level who are
also trying to do the kind of things you want

00:04:01.751 --> 00:04:07.430
to do and you can sort of help each other
and that certainly worked out for me because

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:12.650
I switched from writing features on spec to
I took a class at UCLA Extension Writer’s

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.480
Program where I have taught now in recent
years myself on sitcom writing.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:21.100
I started writing a spec script for the show
FRASIER in that class and then when it was

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:26.180
done I showed it to this friend I’d worked
with on PICKET FENCES but also an aspiring

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:30.160
writer who had just gotten an agent and she
liked the FRASIER and was willing to show

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:31.250
it to her agent.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:34.650
The agent liked the FRASIER enough to want
to sign me.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:39.780
So I got this agent while I was working as
an assistant for Tom Hanks and this agent

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.180
functioned kind of like managers do now.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.210
Managers weren’t such a thing back then
where she was giving me notes.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.880
She’d sign me off but then she’d immediately
wanted me to rewrite it with all these notes

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.691
she gave me before she would send it to anyone.

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:56.840
So then after that was done she had me start
my next one which was MAD ABOUT YOU and after

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.070
that of FRIENDS.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:01.471
So I was like pumping out these sitcom spec
scripts because in those days when you wanted

00:05:01.471 --> 00:05:06.930
to work on staff at a show in the 90’s,
they more valued specs of existing shows.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:11.590
Whereas nowadays it’s original pilots that
writers tend to use for writing samples to

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:16.430
get into TV writing (you know a staff job
like that) which is normally how you get in,

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:20.980
you get hired as a staff writer on a show
if you’re one of the lucky few fortunate

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:21.980
few (fortunate few).

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:27.632
So that was the direction I was headed but
I still had the Tom Hanks great sort of assistant

00:05:27.632 --> 00:05:32.071
day job but I was really starting to think
“Eventually I’m going to be a professional

00:05:32.071 --> 00:05:33.680
writer and I’m going to have an agent.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.150
She’s sending my stuff out.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:38.460
I’ve got some meetings here and there.”

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:44.310
But then things took a funny turn (an amazing
turn) which is that one day Tom Hanks read

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:50.160
one or two of those scripts because his assistant
who I worked kind of under had recommended

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.270
“Oh you should have Tom read your…it would
be fun, whatever.”

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:57.000
I was never going to ask him to read my stuff
or help me in any way because I knew this

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.000
was my job.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.120
He’s not there to help me do that.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.980
And he apparently liked the one or two scripts
he read and decided that I had talent and

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:12.290
he told me my talents were being wasted and
he offered me this amazing life-changing promotion.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:18.340
The week of my 30th birthday, I had a wife
and kid and she was a teacher and I was making

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:22.400
700 dollars a week (I think something like
that?) so it was a really good time to get

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:29.970
a promotion where I was going to help him
basically develop this miniseries he sold

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:34.510
to HBO as executive producer which was FROM
EARTH TO THE MOON which was about the Apollo

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:35.510
Space Program.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:40.480
He’d done the movie Apollo 13 and was a
life long space junkie at at this point was

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:41.870
the biggest star in Hollywood.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:47.260
He’d gotten to that point by then and so
he was able to sell this idea for a miniseries,

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:51.830
a very expensive 12-hour miniseries where
he would dramatize and recreate the entire

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:55.760
Apollo program, right (all these other missions).

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:01.850
So then I helped him kind of figure out the
sort of outline for that and find writers

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:02.850
to write the episodes.

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:07.419
You know, professional screenwriters that
were already established unlike me.

00:07:07.419 --> 00:07:12.950
But eventually I got to write one of the scripts
and then eventually that script became a decent

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.440
script because it took a long time.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:18.419
And then I got asked to help rewrite some
of the other scripts.

00:07:18.419 --> 00:07:22.680
So by the end of that production FROM EARTH
TO THE MOON I had kind of been an apprentice

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:27.350
producer on the whole thing so I got a co-producer
credit on the whole thing and I had a writing

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:32.040
credit or pieces of a writing credit on several
episodes and I just like learned all this

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:35.520
stuff from being on it from beginning to end
and being able to be kind of in the middle

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:40.230
of it because I worked for Tom and he was
the executive producer so I was on set, in

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:44.090
the editing room and did everything or watched
everything.

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:49.030
And so when it won the Emmy for the best miniseries
and all the other big awards, I got to share

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:53.460
in those awards because I was one of the producers
so that was really how I got my start.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:58.340
I was very fortunate in that the first thing
I wrote professionally not only got produced

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.380
which you know most things the writers get
paid to write still don’t get produced,

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:03.380
right?

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:06.500
You can make a whole career writing things
that don’t get produced.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:12.100
Not only to get produced but I was a producer
on it and it won these major awards for being

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:19.860
that so it was kind of a fairy tale beginning
of a career that I owed Tom Hanks and to sitcom

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:25.190
writing which you wouldn’t think translates
into historical drama about the space program

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:30.130
but I somehow managed to figure out how to
write for that series even though it wasn’t

00:08:30.130 --> 00:08:31.300
the kind of stuff I was doing on my own.

